lomatic negotiations with Teodorico Ranieri, who represented Pope Boniface VIII."}, {"sourceSlug": "papacy-medieval", "sourceName": "Medieval Papacy", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "teodorico-ranieri", "targetName": "Teodorico Ranieri", "context": "The political and theological agenda of the Medieval Papacy, particularly under Boniface VIII, profoundly shaped Teodorico Ranieri's career and missions."}], "places": [{"name": "Orvieto, Italy", "role": "Birthplace and early career"}, {"name": "Pisa, Italy", "role": "Archbishopric"}, {"name": "Rome, Papal States", "role": "Cardinalate and Curial Service"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Teodorico Ranieri", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "teodorico-ranieri"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1386355", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teodorico_Ranieri"], "tags": ["Religious Figures", "comparative-religion", "doctrine-development", "cultural-diffusion"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Teodorico Ranieri was a significant figure in papal diplomacy during a critical period, representing the Papacy's efforts to maintain authority against rising national monarchies, though ultimately his missions foreshadowed the Avignon Papacy.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-15T01:23:36Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#27518291065", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Teodorico Ranieri of Orvieto was an Italian cardinal. He was archbishop of Pisa, and bishop of Palestrina.", "newValue": "Teodorico Ranieri of Orvieto, an Italian cardinal who died on December 15, 1306, was a prominent ecclesiastical figure in the turbulent late 13th and early 14th centuries, serving as archbishop of Pisa and cardinal-bishop of Palestrina. His career was deeply intertwined with the increasingly fraught relationship between the Papacy, particularly under Pope Boniface VIII, and the burgeoning power of secular monarchs like Philip IV of France, making him a key actor in the diplomatic struggles that preceded the Avignon Papacy.

Born in Orvieto, Teodorico Ranieri rose through the Church hierarchy, becoming archbishop of Pisa in 1292. His elevation to cardinal-bishop of Palestrina by Pope Boniface VIII in 1298 underscored his reputation as an astute canon lawyer and diplomat. He was dispatched as a crucial papal legate to France between 1299 and 1300, tasked with mediating the escalating conflict between Boniface VIII and King Philip IV over ecclesiastical jurisdiction and taxation. Ranieri also played a significant administrative role during the Jubilee Year of 1300 in Rome, a period of immense spiritual and political importance for the Church.

